"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes Companion, the Steam client module that makes IW4x
appear under its own name in the game activity of the player's Steam
friends. For the command line interface of the \c{companion} program, which
starts the Linux Steam client with Companion, refer to the
\l{companion(1)} man page.

In this document we use the term \i{Companion} (capitalized) to refer to
the code that runs in the Steam client and \i{module} to refer to the file
that contains this code, \c{companion64.dll} on Windows and
\c{libcompanion.so} on Linux. Just \c{companion} refers to the program.
Finally, the \i{title} is the text that IW4x wants Steam friends to see, for
example, \c{IW4x: Modern Warfare 2}.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

Steam shows the player's friends the game that the player is running. For
IW4x this is the Steam app that it runs as, Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2
or Spacewar, or the name of the non-Steam shortcut that started it. Only the
publisher of a game can change how it appears, through the game's Steamworks
settings. Companion lets IW4x set the title itself and change it at any
time while IW4x runs.

How can a module in the Steam client change what friends see? The client
reports the running games to the CM server (the Steam server that the client
stays connected to) in the \c{GamesPlayed} message and the CM server passes
the report on to friends. Companion intercepts this message as the client
sends it and, in each entry that belongs to a running IW4x process, sets the
title that IW4x asked for. The CM server only shows an app that the account
owns, so IW4x also chooses the app to report: Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2
(app \c{10190}) or, if the account doesn't own it, Spacewar (app \c{480}),
which every account owns. Every other message goes out unchanged.

For this to work, IW4x and Companion need to know about each other. IW4x
\i{registers} with Companion by publishing a small record with the app and
the title. Companion, in turn, publishes its status, which tells IW4x
whether Companion is running and whether it managed to hook the Steam
client. We describe this exchange in \l{#protocol Protocol}.

There is a Companion module for each Steam client that IW4x runs with:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{companion64.dll}}

The module for the 64-bit Windows Steam client. IW4x ships it next to
\c{iw4x.dll} and loads it into the running Steam client.|

\li|\c{\b{libcompanion.so}}

The module for the 32-bit native Linux Steam client, which runs IW4x under
Proton. This client only loads the module when it starts, which is what the
\c{companion} program is for.||

Both modules are built from the \c{libcompanion} library, which contains the
parts of Companion that don't depend on the operating system, such as
locating the function to hook and rewriting the reports. As a result, the
library is built and tested on any host. It also contains the vendored
MinHook library. The Windows module uses its hooking engine and both modules
use its instruction decoder.

We begin with a walkthrough that installs Companion on Linux and observes
it at work. The remaining chapters then describe how Companion works in
more detail, starting with what it does inside the Steam client.

\h#intro-linux|Getting Started on Linux|

On Windows there is nothing to install since IW4x loads Companion into the
Steam client every time it starts. So let's set up Companion on Linux.

The Linux module requires glibc 2.36 or later (Debian 12, for example). To
build it we need GCC 16 or later together with the 32-bit glibc and
libstdc++ development files. The \c{companion} program also needs the static
libstdc++ library. On Fedora, for example, these come with the
\c{glibc-devel.i686}, \c{libstdc++-devel.i686}, and \c{libstdc++-static}
packages.

The Steam client is a 32-bit program and the programs that start it are
64-bit. So we install Companion from two build configurations into the same
location. The i386 configuration provides the module and the x86_64
configuration provides the \c{companion} program along with the module's
empty 64-bit stand-ins. We will see what the stand-ins are for in
\l{#linux-preload Preloading}.

Let's create the two configurations. The installation filter limits the
installation to what the Steam client needs and leaves out the library's
headers and \c{pkg-config} files:

\
$ f='lib/companion/@true lib/@false include/@false'

$ bpkg create -d companion-i686 cc   \\
  config.cxx='g++ -m32'            \\
  config.cxx.target=i686-linux-gnu \\
  config.c='gcc -m32'              \\
  config.c.target=i686-linux-gnu   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"

$ bpkg create -d companion-x86_64 cc \\
  config.cxx=g++                   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"
\

\N|GCC only reports the i386 target that \c{-m32} selects if it is built
with multiarch support (Debian, Ubuntu), which is why we specify the target
explicitly.|

Next we build and install the module in the i386 configuration and the
program together with the stand-ins in the x86_64 configuration:

\
$ bpkg build -d companion-i686 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main
$ bpkg install -d companion-i686 libcompanion

$ bpkg build -d companion-x86_64 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main \\
  companion
$ bpkg install -d companion-x86_64 libcompanion companion
\

Now we can start the Steam client with Companion. The client only loads
the module at startup, so if it is already running, then \c{companion} shuts
it down and starts it again:

\
$ companion --steam
\

\N|The arguments after \c{--} are passed to the Steam command, for example,
\c{companion --steam -- -silent}. See \l{companion(1)} for details.|

Companion writes its diagnostics to the Steam client's \c{stderr}, which
the client copies to its console log. Let's see what we've got:

\
$ grep -o '\\[companion\\].*' ~/.steam/steam/logs/console-linux.txt
[companion] send function at steamclient.so+0x2dca890 (1 references)
[companion] hooked the send function (9 bytes relocated)
[companion] active (build 2)
\

The first line tells us that Companion found the function through which the
Steam client sends its messages, at this offset in \c{steamclient.so}. The
second line says that Companion hooked this function and the last one that
it is active. From this point on Companion is idle until IW4x registers.

Let's now start IW4x and look at the log again:

\
[companion] record changed
[companion] resent GamesPlayed on connection 0xc959a800 (304 -> 326
  bytes, after a frame on 0xdcf81660)
\

Notice that Companion resent the report. Steam reports a game as soon as it
starts the game process, which is before IW4x has a chance to register with
Companion. As a result, the first report goes out unchanged and our friends
see the plain app or shortcut name. Steam then only sends a new report when
the set of running games changes. So Companion keeps the last report and,
once IW4x publishes its record (the \c{record changed} line), rewrites this
report and sends it again (see \l{#operation-resend Report Resending} for
details).

The two sizes in the second line are those of the report before and after
the rewrite, with the difference being the title. From now on our friends
see the title that we asked for.

\h1#operation|Operation|

Now that we have seen Companion at work, let's look at what it does inside
the Steam client. This part is the same on both platforms. We will see how
each module gets into the Steam client and how it finds the record of IW4x
in the next two chapters.

\h#operation-startup|Startup|

Companion starts on a thread of its own as soon as the module is loaded
into the Steam client. The first thing it does is publish its status with
the \c{starting} state. Publishing the status also makes sure that there is
a single Companion per Steam client since a second copy of the module finds
the status already published and stays inert.

Next, Companion looks for the send function in the Steam client library,
which is \c{steamclient64.dll} on Windows and \c{steamclient.so} on Linux,
and hooks it. It then starts watching the records for changes and publishes
the \c{active} state, which is the \c{active (build 2)} line that we saw in
the console log.

If Companion doesn't recognize the Steam client library, then it publishes
the \c{unsupported} state. If it fails to hook the send function, then it
publishes the \c{failed} state. In both cases it does nothing further and
the Steam client continues as if the module was never loaded.

\h#operation-locate|Locating the Send Function|

The Steam client sends every message to the CM server through the
\c{CWebSocketConnection::BBuildAndAsyncSendFrame()} function. The Steam
client library doesn't export this function and its machine code changes
with almost every Steam build, which rules out a byte signature. How does
Companion find it? It relies on how the function is written.

The function opens a profiler scope named after itself, which means that
the library contains the function's name as a string and that the function
loads the address of this string. We call this string the \i{anchor}. Let's
see what this looks like in the \c{steamclient.so} that we hooked in
\l{#intro-linux Getting Started on Linux}. The anchor is in \c{.rodata} at
\c{0xdf7ed4} and the send function at \c{0x2dca890} references it like
this (the addresses are relative to the library's load address):

\
2dcaae6:  lea    eax,[edi-0x2152478]
2dcaaec:  push   0x6
2dcaaee:  push   eax
2dcaaef:  call   e92ac0
\

The \c{lea} is \c{0x256} bytes into the function, on an out-of-line path
that a branch near the start of the function jumps to. Notice also that it
doesn't contain the anchor's address. Position-independent i386 code has no
PC-relative data addressing, so it addresses data relative to the global
offset table (GOT) and keeps the GOT's address in a register, here \c{edi}.
The function loads this register in its prologue:

\
2dca890:  push   ebp
2dca891:  mov    ebp,esp
2dca893:  push   edi
2dca894:  call   e9b6eb      # mov edi,[esp]; ret
2dca899:  add    edi,0x17fab3
\

The call to the PC thunk stores the return address, \c{0x2dca899}, in
\c{edi} and the \c{add} turns it into the GOT's address, \c{0x2f4a34c}. The
\c{lea} then subtracts \c{0x2152478} from it, which gives us the anchor's
address, \c{0xdf7ed4}.

Companion reproduces this arithmetic on the library image. It first finds
the anchor in the non-executable segments together with its terminating
\c{NUL}, which excludes longer names that start with the anchor. It then
scans the code for the GOT loads that GCC and Clang generate. GCC calls a PC
thunk and adds the distance to the GOT, as in the listing above, while Clang
calls the next instruction and pops the return address before the \c{add}.
Companion matches these byte sequences at any offset, which requires no
instruction decoding, and takes the GOT's address from the first one that
is inside a function. Finally, it scans the code for \c{lea r32, [r32 +
disp32]} (opcode \c{0x8d} with a ModRM byte of mod \c{10}) whose
displacement added to the GOT's address gives the anchor's address.

Each such reference leads to the function that contains it, which Companion
looks up in the binary search table of the unwind information
(\c{.eh_frame_hdr}). The table only contains the start of each function and
the end comes from the function's FDE. Of the referencing functions
Companion keeps the ones that load the GOT themselves, since only in those
is the register guaranteed to hold the GOT's address.

The 64-bit Windows client library is simpler in this respect. There the
reference is a RIP-relative \c{lea r64, [rip + disp32]} (a \c{REX.W} prefix,
opcode \c{0x8d}, and a ModRM byte of mod \c{00} and r/m \c{101}) and the
displacement is relative to the end of the instruction. Companion maps each
reference to its function through the exception directory (\c{.pdata}),
following chained entries to the function's primary entry. Functions that
inline the send function load the anchor as well, further from their entry.
So Companion keeps the function that loads it within \c{0x100} bytes of its
entry, right after its prologue.

The search must end with a single function. Any other result means a Steam
build that Companion doesn't recognize, in which case it publishes the
\c{unsupported} state. The diagnostics include the outcome together with the
numbers of references and candidates that the search found, for example:

\
[companion] unable to locate the send function: ambiguous (4
  references, 2 candidates)
\

The outcome has one of the following values:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{no-anchor}}

The library contains no anchor, which means that this build has no profiler
scopes.|

\li|\c{\b{no-got}}

No function loads the GOT (i386 only).|

\li|\c{\b{no-reference}}

Nothing references the anchor.|

\li|\c{\b{no-candidate}}

No referencing function passes the architecture's test.|

\li|\c{\b{ambiguous}}

There are more than eight anchors or more than one candidate function.||

\h#operation-hook|Send Hook|

To intercept the messages, Companion hooks the send function with a
\i{detour}. It overwrites the start of the function with a jump to its own
code, which calls the original function through a \i{trampoline}. The
trampoline contains the overwritten instructions followed by a jump to the
first instruction after them. On Windows Companion installs the detour with
the MinHook hooking engine. On Linux it builds its own i386 detour, which we
look at in \l{#linux-detour Detour}.

The hook runs on the Steam client's network thread for every message that
the client sends, so we want the common case to be cheap. Each message, or
\i{frame}, starts with an 8-byte header that consists of the message type
(\c{EMsg}) with the protobuf flag (\c{0x80000000}) and the size of the
protobuf header that follows. The hook compares the message type to the
three types that carry a \c{GamesPlayed} report (\c{715}, \c{742}, and
\c{5410}) and passes every other frame to the original function as is.

\h#operation-rewrite|Report Rewriting|

A \c{GamesPlayed} report contains a \c{GamePlayed} entry for each running
game. The entry includes the game id (field \c{2}) and the id of the process
that Steam tracks for this game (field \c{9}). Newer Steam versions also
send the ids of the whole process tree (field \c{32}). For each entry
Companion looks for a record that IW4x published for one of these processes
(see \l{#protocol Protocol}). If there is one, then Companion sets the game
id to the app from the record and the game's extra information (field
\c{7}) to the title from the record. The CM server shows friends this extra
information as the title.

Companion writes such an entry with the old game id and extra information
removed and the new values appended and copies the rest of the frame byte
for byte. If none of the entries belongs to a registered IW4x process, then
the frame goes out unchanged, as does a frame larger than 8192 bytes, which
is far beyond what Steam sends in practice.

\h#operation-resend|Report Resending|

Recall the console log from \l{#intro-linux Getting Started on Linux}, where
Steam reported IW4x before IW4x registered and Companion resent the report
once the record appeared. Let's see why this is necessary and how it works.

Steam reports a game right after starting it, normally before IW4x
publishes its record. After that Steam only sends a new report when the set
of running games changes. IW4x can also update its record while it runs, for
example, to switch from Modern Warfare 2 to Spacewar. So if Companion only
rewrote the reports that Steam sends, friends would not see the title until
the set of running games changed.

To handle this, Companion keeps the last report together with the frame
that it actually sent for it and watches the records for changes. On a
change it rewrites the report again and, if the result differs from what the
CM server last received, sends it on the report's connection.

When does the resend happen? Companion has no thread of its own in the
Steam client's networking code, so it resends the report when the client
sends its next frame from the thread that sent the last report. This frame
can go out on another connection, as it did in our console log. Steam uses
a single thread per connection, which means that the resend never
interleaves with the client's own frames on the report's connection. If
that connection has been idle for 60 seconds, then Companion assumes it is
closed and drops the report.

\h1#linux|Linux Module|

The native Linux Steam client runs Windows games with Proton, normally in a
container of the Steam Linux Runtime. In this chapter we look at how
Companion gets into this Steam client and hooks its send function. We then
see how it finds the record of IW4x running in such a container.

\h#linux-preload|Preloading|

The Steam client doesn't load plugins, so the module has to be preloaded
with \c{LD_PRELOAD} when the client starts. This is the job of the
\c{companion} program that we used in \l{#intro-linux Getting Started on
Linux}. Let's look at the value that it sets:

\
LD_PRELOAD=/usr/local/lib/companion/$PLATFORM/libcompanion.so
\

A chain of 64-bit programs starts the Steam client and they all inherit
\c{LD_PRELOAD}. A 64-bit program cannot load the 32-bit module and its
dynamic linker would warn about every library that it fails to preload. This
is what the \c{$PLATFORM} token is for. The dynamic linker expands it in each
process separately. In the 32-bit Steam client it expands to \c{i686}, the
directory that contains the module. In a 64-bit program it expands to
\c{x86_64} or, on some CPUs, to a more specific name such as \c{haswell}, and
each of these directories contains an empty 64-bit stand-in that the program
loads without a warning.

The dynamic linker also splits \c{LD_PRELOAD} at spaces and colons and
provides no way to escape them. If the module directory contains either, for
example, because it is inside the IW4x installation, then \c{companion}
preloads the module through a symbolic link to this directory that it
creates in \c{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}.

Note that the module removes itself from \c{LD_PRELOAD} in every process
that it is loaded into, so the processes that this process starts, such as
Proton and IW4x, don't load it. In any process other than the Steam client,
which the module recognizes by its executable name (\c{steam}), this is the
only thing that the module does.

\h#linux-startup|Startup|

On Linux Companion publishes its status in the \c{/dev/shm/companion.}\i{uid}
file, where \i{uid} is the user id, and keeps this file locked while it runs.
If we start a second Steam client as the same user, then its Companion fails
to lock the file and stays inert:

\
[companion] another companion is resident, staying inert
\

The Steam client loads \c{steamclient.so} once its bootstrapper finishes
updating it, which can take a while on a slow connection. So Companion waits
up to 30 minutes for the library to load before it gives up and publishes
the \c{unsupported} state.

When the Steam client exits, Companion removes the status file. If the
client crashes, then the file stays behind until the next Companion takes it
over. In the meantime IW4x recognizes the status as stale by the Steam
client's process id that it contains.

\h#linux-detour|Detour|

The Linux Steam client is a 32-bit program, so Companion builds an i386
detour of its own. Let's see what it does with the prologue of the send
function that we looked at in \l{#operation-locate Locating the Send
Function}. The detour jump (\c{jmp rel32}) takes 5 bytes. So Companion
decodes instructions with the MinHook instruction decoder until it has
covered at least as many and moves them into the trampoline. Here these are
the first four instructions, 9 bytes in total, which is the \c{9 bytes
relocated} line in our console log. The trampoline for them looks like this,
with \i{base} being the library's load address:

\
push   ebp
mov    ebp,esp
push   edi
mov    edi,base+0x2dca899
jmp    base+0x2dca899
\

On i386 only control transfer instructions depend on where they run, so
Companion copies every other instruction unchanged. The call to the PC thunk
is such a control transfer since, in the trampoline, it would store the
trampoline's address in \c{edi}. So Companion replaces it with a \c{mov} of
the same size that stores the original return address directly. Similarly,
for the Clang sequence it replaces the call to the next instruction with a
\c{push} of the return address. As a result, the trampoline is always as
large as the overwritten instructions plus the jump back, which continues
with the \c{add} at \c{0x2dca899}.

Companion refuses to hook the function if the overwritten instructions
contain any other control transfer or if a direct branch in the function
targets one of them other than the first, since such a branch would land
inside the jump. It also refuses any instruction that the decoder doesn't
fully understand.

Other threads can be entering the send function while Companion hooks it.
So Companion writes the jump with a single \c{lock cmpxchg8b} of the aligned
8 bytes that contain it, which means that a thread sees either the original
bytes or the complete jump. The linkers align functions to 16 bytes, so the
jump always fits into one such quadword. If the compare-exchange finds
different bytes than the ones that Companion built the trampoline from,
then something else is patching the function and Companion leaves it alone:

\
[companion] unable to hook the send function: it changed while hooking
\

The trampoline gets a page of its own, which is writable while Companion
fills it and executable afterwards, never both at once. Note also that the
hook realigns the stack to 16 bytes on entry. The code that GCC generates
for the i386 System V ABI assumes this alignment and the Steam client's code
doesn't necessarily preserve it.

\h#linux-lookup|IW4x Lookup|

The native Steam client cannot see the Windows objects of IW4x running
under Wine. So IW4x publishes its record as a file in \c{/dev/shm}, which the
Proton container shares with the host. Companion watches this directory with
\c{inotify} and treats a write, removal, or rename of a record file as a
change. The question then is which file belongs to which report.

Steam reports a game with the id of the process that it tracks for the
game, which is one of Steam's own helpers or one of Proton's processes. IW4x
has no way to find out its own Linux process id since Wine performs its file
operations in the \c{wineserver} process of its prefix. So IW4x names its
record after the process id of this \c{wineserver}.

To find the record for a report, Companion searches the reported process and
its descendants breadth-first. For each process it tries the record under
each of the process's ids, one per process id namespace that the process is
in, since IW4x sees the ids of its container. The record also contains the
start time of the \c{wineserver}, which lets Companion tell a current record
from a stale one that an earlier process with the same id left behind.

A game under Proton consists of a few dozen processes. The search visits at
most 256 of them and issues diagnostics if it runs out of processes to
visit:

\
[companion] no record for process 4242 (overflow)
\

Companion also issues diagnostics if the reported process is the Steam
client or one of its ancestors (\c{related}). The tree of such a process
contains every game, so Companion doesn't search it.

In the Steam Linux Runtime container the \c{wineserver} is a descendant of
the reported process. Without the container the \c{wineserver} daemonizes
and leaves the tree, and so does IW4x. The reported process, Proton's
\c{steam.exe}, uses the same Wine prefix, which Proton passes to it in the
\c{WINEPREFIX} environment variable. So if the tree has no record, then
Companion tries the record of the \c{wineserver} of this prefix. To find this
\c{wineserver}, it uses the server directory that Wine names after the
device and inode numbers of the prefix directory, for example:

\
/tmp/.wine-1000/server-34-8186e/lock
\

The \c{wineserver} keeps the \c{lock} file in this directory locked while it
runs and Companion tries the record of the process that holds this lock.

\h#linux-glibc|glibc Compatibility|

The module runs in the Steam client and therefore with the glibc of the
system. So we link the C++ runtime statically and bind the module's glibc
references to versions no newer than 2.36, which lets a single build run on
any distribution with glibc 2.36 or later. We build the \c{companion}
program the same way and the tests of both check this bound.

\h1#windows|Windows Module|

On Windows the Steam client is a 64-bit program and IW4x is a 32-bit one. A
32-bit process cannot load a module into a 64-bit process, so IW4x uses the
64-bit \c{rundll32.exe} to attach Companion to the Steam client. In this
chapter we look at how this works and how the module starts in the Steam
client.

\h#windows-attach|Attaching|

To attach Companion, IW4x runs the module's \c{companion_attach} entry point
in \c{rundll32.exe} and passes it the Steam client's process id, for
example:

\
rundll32.exe \"...\\companion64.dll\",companion_attach 5120
\

The entry point verifies that the process is a 64-bit Steam client and loads
the module into it by calling \c{LoadLibraryW()} on a remote thread. It then
waits for Companion to settle and returns the outcome as the
\c{rundll32.exe} exit code, which has one of the following values:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} active}

Companion is active.|

\li|\c{\b{1} unsupported}

Companion doesn't recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{2} failed}

Companion failed to hook the send function.|

\li|\c{\b{3} invalid_arguments}

The command line doesn't contain a valid process id.|

\li|\c{\b{4} not_steam}

The process is not a 64-bit Steam client.|

\li|\c{\b{5} access_denied}

The process cannot be opened, for example, because it runs with higher
privileges.|

\li|\c{\b{6} injection_failed}

The module could not be loaded into the process.|

\li|\c{\b{7} timeout}

The module was not loaded within 10 seconds or Companion did not settle
within 5 seconds.||

If Companion is already running in the Steam client, for example, from an
earlier IW4x session, then the entry point reports its state and loads
nothing.

\h#windows-startup|Startup|

On Windows Companion publishes its status in a named file mapping whose name
includes the Steam client's process id. Creating this mapping is also what
makes sure that there is a single Companion per Steam client. Companion then
finds the send function in \c{steamclient64.dll} and continues as described
in \l{#operation-startup Startup}.

\N|The Steam client loads \c{steamclient64.dll} before any game can start.
If the library is not loaded, then Companion publishes the \c{unsupported}
state.|

Once the hook is installed, the Steam client executes the module's code, so
the module stays loaded until the client exits.

IW4x publishes its record in a named file mapping of its own and sets a
named event after every change to the record, which wakes up Companion's
record watcher.

Companion writes its diagnostics to the debugger output, where each line
starts with \c{[companion64]}. We can view them with a tool such as
DebugView.

\h1#protocol|Protocol|

In this chapter we describe the protocol that IW4x and Companion use to
communicate. It is declared in \c{<libcompanion/protocol.hxx>}, which only
depends on the standard library so that IW4x can include it directly.

\h#protocol-compat|Compatibility|

IW4x and the two modules are each built with their own toolchain for their
own architecture and upgraded on their own schedule. Companion also stays
loaded until the Steam client exits. As a result, any version of IW4x can
meet any version of Companion.

This is why every structure of the protocol has a fixed layout of 32-bit
fields and starts with a magic followed by its version and size. A reader
only accepts the layout that it knows and every change to a layout
increments its version. Companion also publishes its build number, which we
increment with every change in Companion's behavior that IW4x can observe.
The current build is 2.

\h#protocol-record|Registration Record|

IW4x registers by publishing the following record, which contains the app
to report and the title to show:

\
struct record
{
  uint32_t magic;          // 0x50345749 (\"IW4P\")
  uint32_t version;        // 1
  uint32_t size;           // 84
  uint32_t process_id;     // IW4x process id.
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64]; // Title, UTF-8, NUL-terminated.
};
\

The app id must be non-zero and fit into 24 bits. The title must be valid
UTF-8 without control characters. It must also be non-empty and fit into the
64 bytes together with the terminating \c{NUL}. IW4x can update both while
it runs. For example, it starts with Modern Warfare 2 (app \c{10190}) and
switches to Spacewar (app \c{480}) if Steam reports that the account doesn't
own it.

On Windows IW4x publishes the record in the
\c{Local\\Companion.SteamPresence.}\i{pid} named file mapping, where \i{pid}
is the IW4x process id, which the record repeats. The mapping exists while
IW4x holds a handle to it, so the record goes away with IW4x. IW4x writes the
magic last with release ordering and Companion reads it first with acquire
ordering, so a record with the right magic is always complete. After every
change to the record IW4x sets the
\c{Local\\Companion.SteamPresence.Changed.}\i{steam-pid} auto-reset event,
where \i{steam-pid} is the Steam client's process id.

On Linux IW4x publishes the record in the
\c{/dev/shm/companion-presence.}\i{pid} file, where \i{pid} is the process
id of the IW4x \c{wineserver} (see \l{#linux-lookup IW4x Lookup}). IW4x opens
this file through Wine's \c{\\\\?\\unix\\} path prefix and writes it in one
go. The Linux record has two more fields that hold the \c{wineserver} start
time, which is field 22 of \c{/proc/}\i{pid}\c{/stat}:

\
struct unix_record
{
  uint32_t magic;           // 0x55345749 (\"IW4U\")
  uint32_t version;         // 1
  uint32_t size;            // 92
  uint32_t process_id;      // wineserver process id.
  uint32_t start_time_low;  // wineserver start time.
  uint32_t start_time_high;
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64];
};
\

\h#protocol-status|Companion Status|

Companion publishes its status, which IW4x reads to find out whether
Companion is running and in which state. The state has one of the following
values:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} starting}

Companion is initializing.|

\li|\c{\b{1} active}

The send hook is installed and the records are applied.|

\li|\c{\b{2} unsupported}

Companion doesn't recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{3} failed}

Companion failed to hook the send function.||

On Windows Companion publishes the status in the
\c{Local\\Companion.Steam.}\i{steam-pid} named file mapping, where
\i{steam-pid} is the Steam client's process id. Companion stores the state
last with release ordering and IW4x loads it first with acquire ordering:

\
struct status
{
  uint32_t magic;   // 0x53345749 (\"IW4S\")
  uint32_t version; // 1
  uint32_t size;    // 24
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;   // Companion build.
  uint32_t hook;    // Send function offset or 0.
};
\

On Linux Companion publishes the status in the
\c{/dev/shm/companion.}\i{uid} file, where \i{uid} is the real user id,
which IW4x reads from \c{/proc/self/status}. The Linux status also contains
the Steam client's process id, which lets IW4x recognize a status that a
crashed client left behind:

\
struct unix_status
{
  uint32_t magic;     // 0x54345749 (\"IW4T\")
  uint32_t version;   // 1
  uint32_t size;      // 28
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;
  uint32_t hook;
  uint32_t steam_pid; // Steam client process id.
};
\
"
